---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { getGuide } from "../../data/guides";

const guide = getGuide("configuration");
---

<DocsLayout guide={guide}>
<section class="docs-hero docs-searchable" id="overview" data-search="configuration trusted yaml custom rules suppressions version one">
          <p class="docs-kicker">Trusted configuration</p>
          <h1>Make policy explicit.<span>Keep review narrow.</span></h1>
          <p>
            Use a reviewed YAML file when your organization needs an extra detector or has a specific, documented exception. Configuration is opt in: repyy
            never trusts a
            <code>.repyy.yaml</code> supplied by the repository being scanned.
          </p>
          <div class="docs-summary" aria-label="Configuration summary">
            <span><b>Version 1</b> Required top-level version</span><span><b>External</b> Pass the file with <code>--config</code></span
            ><span><b>Declarative</b> Rules match text; they cannot execute commands</span>
          </div>
          <div class="docs-callout">
            <strong>Keep the trusted file outside the target</strong>
            <p>
              Store your policy in a controlled location, review changes like code, and pass it explicitly:
              <code>repyy scan ./checkout --config ./policy/repyy.yaml</code>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="boundary" data-search="trust boundary repository owned config weaken scan external opt in">
          <h2>Understand the trust boundary</h2>
          <p>
            A repository cannot weaken its own scan with a repository-owned configuration file. Only the path you explicitly provide with
            <code>--config</code> is loaded. This lets you inspect an unfamiliar repository while keeping policy under your control.
          </p>
          <p>
            Configuration adds custom rules and active suppressions. It does not remove built-in rules, change unrelated findings, or make source execution
            safe.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="minimal" data-search="minimal yaml version rules suppressions empty file">
          <h2>Start with valid YAML</h2>
          <p>The top-level <code>version</code> must be the integer <code>1</code>. Start with both lists present, even when they are empty.</p>
          <pre class="docs-example" aria-label="Minimal repyy YAML">
version: 1
rules: []
suppressions: []</pre
          >
          <div class="docs-command">
            <div><span>Save as policy.yaml, then validate</span><button type="button" data-copy="repyy rules validate policy.yaml">Copy</button></div>
            <code>repyy rules validate policy.yaml</code>
          </div>
          <p>
            Validation checks YAML syntax, the version, rule fields, regular expressions, suppression fingerprints, and suppression dates. It does not scan a
            target.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="workflow" data-search="workflow validate scan config command steps">
          <h2>Use the safe workflow</h2>
          <div class="workflow-list">
            <article>
              <b>1</b>
              <div>
                <strong>Write the smallest policy</strong>
                <p>Add only a rule or exception you can explain and review.</p>
              </div>
            </article>
            <article>
              <b>2</b>
              <div>
                <strong>Validate before scanning</strong>
                <p>Run <code>repyy rules validate policy.yaml</code> and fix every error.</p>
              </div>
            </article>
            <article>
              <b>3</b>
              <div>
                <strong>Pass it explicitly</strong>
                <p>Run the target with <code>--config policy.yaml</code>, then inspect the evidence and coverage.</p>
              </div>
            </article>
          </div>
          <div class="docs-command">
            <div>
              <span>Complete workflow</span
              ><button
                type="button"
                data-copy="repyy rules validate policy.yaml; repyy scan ./checkout --config policy.yaml --format html --output review.html"
              >
                Copy
              </button>
            </div>
            <code>repyy rules validate policy.yaml repyy scan ./checkout --config policy.yaml --format html --output review.html</code>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section
          class="docs-section docs-searchable"
          id="rule-fields"
          data-search="custom rule fields required optional id category severity confidence description pattern globs remediation rationale legitimate disposition downgrade"
        >
          <h2>Custom rule fields</h2>
          <p>
            Every custom rule requires <code>id</code>, <code>category</code>, <code>severity</code>, <code>confidence</code>, <code>description</code>, and
            <code>pattern</code>. The remaining fields add context or narrow matching.
          </p>
          <div class="docs-table compact" role="table" aria-label="Custom rule fields">
            <div role="row">
              <b role="cell">Required</b
              ><span role="cell"
                ><code>id</code>, <code>category</code>, <code>severity</code>, <code>confidence</code>, <code>description</code>, <code>pattern</code></span
              >
            </div>
            <div role="row">
              <b role="cell">Matching</b
              ><span role="cell"><code>globs</code> limits file paths; <code>match_scope</code> is <code>raw</code> (default) or <code>code</code>.</span>
            </div>
            <div role="row">
              <b role="cell">Review context</b
              ><span role="cell"><code>remediation</code>, <code>rationale</code>, <code>legitimate_use</code>, and <code>disposition</code>.</span>
            </div>
            <div role="row">
              <b role="cell">Advanced</b><span role="cell"><code>allow_context_downgrade</code> controls whether context can lower confidence.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            <code>severity</code> is <code>low</code>, <code>medium</code>, <code>high</code>, or <code>critical</code>. <code>confidence</code> is
            <code>low</code>, <code>medium</code>, or <code>high</code>. <code>disposition</code> is <code>informational</code>, <code>harden</code>,
            <code>review</code>, or <code>block</code>; it changes presentation.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="rule-walkthrough" data-search="rule walkthrough regex pattern globs organization policy yaml example">
          <h2>Walk through a custom rule</h2>
          <p>
            This example flags an unapproved bootstrap host only in shell and PowerShell files. The pattern is a regular expression, so punctuation that has
            regex meaning must be escaped.
          </p>
          <pre class="docs-example" aria-label="Custom rule YAML">
version: 1
rules:
  - id: ORG-001
    category: organization-policy
    severity: high
    confidence: medium
    description: Unapproved bootstrap host
    pattern: 'bootstrap\.example\.invalid'
    globs: ['*.sh', '*.ps1']
    match_scope: raw
    disposition: review
    rationale: Our policy requires approval for bootstrap hosts.
    legitimate_use: A reviewed internal host may be expected.
    remediation: Confirm the host with the repository owner.</pre
          >
          <p>
            <code>globs</code> applies to file names or paths using case-insensitive comparisons. <code>raw</code> checks comments and strings as well as code;
            use <code>code</code> when only executable code should match. Custom rules cannot use <code>structured</code> scope, which is reserved for built-in
            structured detectors.
          </p>
          <div class="docs-command">
            <div>
              <span>Validate then use it</span
              ><button type="button" data-copy="repyy rules validate policy.yaml; repyy scan ./repo --config policy.yaml">Copy</button>
            </div>
            <code>repyy rules validate policy.yaml repyy scan ./repo --config policy.yaml</code>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="scope" data-search="match scope raw code structured custom rule comments strings executable">
          <h2>Choose the matching scope</h2>
          <div class="docs-table compact" role="table" aria-label="Rule matching scopes">
            <div role="row">
              <b role="cell"><code>raw</code></b
              ><span role="cell">Default for custom rules. Matches the raw file, including comments and strings.</span>
            </div>
            <div role="row">
              <b role="cell"><code>code</code></b
              ><span role="cell">Restricts a custom rule to executable code according to the scanner’s code extraction.</span>
            </div>
            <div role="row">
              <b role="cell"><code>structured</code></b
              ><span role="cell">Reserved for built-in structured detectors; rejected in custom configuration.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            Start with <code>raw</code> when the policy concerns a literal anywhere in a file. Choose <code>code</code> when comments, examples, or
            documentation would create noise. Test the choice against representative files before adopting the rule.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section
          class="docs-section docs-searchable"
          id="suppressions"
          data-search="suppressions fingerprint reason expires iso date yaml finding json reviewed exception"
        >
          <h2>Suppress one reviewed finding</h2>
          <p>
            Suppressions are tied to a finding’s stable fingerprint. Save a JSON scan, copy the exact fingerprint, record why the match is acceptable, and add
            the exception to the trusted file.
          </p>
          <div class="docs-command">
            <div><span>Save JSON evidence</span><button type="button" data-copy="repyy scan ./repo --format json --output scan.json">Copy</button></div>
            <code>repyy scan ./repo --format json --output scan.json</code>
          </div>
          <p>
            Every suppression needs <code>fingerprint</code> and <code>reason</code>. <code>expires</code> is optional and must be an ISO calendar date in
            <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code> form. Once expired, the suppression stops being active automatically.
          </p>
          <pre class="docs-example" aria-label="Suppression YAML">
version: 1
suppressions:
  - fingerprint: 'sha256:replace-with-report-fingerprint'
    reason: 'Reviewed internal fixture; no executable path.'
    expires: '2026-12-31'</pre
          >
          <p>A suppression does not remove the underlying rule or change unrelated findings. Keep the scope narrow and re-review it before its expiry.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="review-example" data-search="suppression example review finding fingerprint workflow complete yaml">
          <h2>Review example: from finding to exception</h2>
          <div class="workflow-list">
            <article>
              <b>1</b>
              <div>
                <strong>Read the location</strong>
                <p>Open the reported file and inspect the matched context. Confirm whether it is executable, test, fixture, or documentation content.</p>
              </div>
            </article>
            <article>
              <b>2</b>
              <div>
                <strong>Copy the exact fingerprint</strong>
                <p>Use the JSON report so the stable <code>sha256:...</code> value is copied without transcription errors.</p>
              </div>
            </article>
            <article>
              <b>3</b>
              <div>
                <strong>Write a reason and expiry</strong>
                <p>Record the reviewer’s decision in YAML. An expiry keeps temporary exceptions from becoming permanent.</p>
              </div>
            </article>
            <article>
              <b>4</b>
              <div>
                <strong>Validate and rescan</strong>
                <p>Run validation, then scan again and confirm only the reviewed finding changed.</p>
              </div>
            </article>
          </div>
          <div class="docs-note">
            <strong>Do not suppress to get green CI</strong>
            <p>Use a suppression only when the evidence supports the exception. If the behavior is not acceptable, fix or remove it instead.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section
          class="docs-section docs-searchable"
          id="limits"
          data-search="errors invalid version regex suppression date exit code 3 configuration validate"
        >
          <h2>Limits and validation errors</h2>
          <p>
            Configuration errors return exit code <code>3</code>. Common causes are a version other than <code>1</code>, malformed YAML, an invalid regular
            expression, <code>structured</code> match scope on a custom rule, an unsupported enum value, or a suppression missing its fingerprint, reason, or
            valid date.
          </p>
          <p>
            Fix the YAML and rerun <code>repyy rules validate</code> before scanning. A valid configuration does not guarantee that a target scan will complete;
            timeouts, permissions, file limits, archive limits, or sandbox failures still produce incomplete coverage and exit code <code>2</code>.
          </p>
          <div class="docs-command">
            <div><span>Validate only</span><button type="button" data-copy="repyy rules validate policy.yaml">Copy</button></div>
            <code>repyy rules validate policy.yaml</code>
          </div>
          <p>
            For the complete command list, see the <a href="../cli/">CLI reference ↗</a>. For installation, reports, Docker, and the trust model, return to the
            <a href="../docs/">documentation home ↗</a>.
          </p>
        </section>
</DocsLayout>
